<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Anglo-Afghan War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Anglo-Afghan War was fought from 1878 to 1880 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, after the emir Sher Ali Khan received a Russian mission. British forces occupied much of the country and imposed the Treaty of Gandamak in 1879, but the murder of the British envoy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and the defeat at Maiwand prolonged the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> withdrew after recognising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a> as emir, keeping control of Afghan foreign policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war.jpg" alt="Second Anglo-Afghan War" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AfghanTsakhtan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:9th_Regiment_at_Ali_Masjid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a> between the British and Russian empires. Tension between them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in June 1878, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> then turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. It sent an uninvited mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, which arrived on 22 July 1878, and Sher Ali Khan tried unsuccessfully to keep it out. On 14 August the British demanded that he accept a British mission as well. The viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Lord Lytton, sent one in September 1878, but it was turned back at the eastern entrance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a>, and a deadline of 20 November was set.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first campaign began in November 1878, when about 50,000 British fighting men, mostly Indians, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in three columns. Victories at Ali Masjid and Peiwar Kotal left the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> virtually undefended. Sher Ali left for Mazar-i-Sharif, was refused entry to Russian territory and died there on 21 February 1879. His son Yaqub Khan signed the Treaty of Gandamak on 26 May 1879, giving up control of Afghan foreign affairs, ceding Quetta and areas of the North-West Frontier, and receiving an annual subsidy of 600,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. On 3 September 1879 an uprising in Kabul killed the British envoy Sir Louis Cavagnari with his guards and staff.</p><p>Major General Sir Frederick Roberts defeated the Afghan army at Charasiab on 6 October and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> two days later. Yaqub Khan, suspected of complicity, abdicated, and an uprising besieged the Sherpur Cantonment in December before collapsing on 23 December. Meanwhile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a>, long in exile in Turkestan, was allowed by the Russians to re-enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, took Badakhshan and Afghan Turkestan, and in July 1880 was told that the British would recognise him as ruler. That month <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, rebelled, defeated a British detachment at the Battle of Maiwand and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>. Roberts defeated him on 1 September 1880 at the Battle of Kandahar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a> beaten, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a> ruled as emir and was seen as the real victor. The British returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, gave up the plan of a resident in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, and handled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>'s foreign policy in return for a subsidy and protection, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> kept internal rule and a buffer against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The war cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> some 17 million pounds, about three times the estimate, and about 19.5 million by the withdrawal in March 1881. Abdur Rahman governed autocratically and became known as the Iron Amir. In 1893 Mortimer Durand reached the Durand Line Agreement with him on 12 November, fixing the limits of their spheres of influence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Anglo-Afghan War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…largest military force in Asia under a European power and served British interests far beyond India: in the <b>Second Anglo-Afghan War</b> of 1878–1880, in the Third Anglo-Burmese War of 1885, which led to the annexation of Upper Burma, in repeated campaigns on the North-West Frontier, and in Egypt,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Second Anglo-Afghan War</b> of 1878 to 1880, Abdur Rahman Khan, the 'Iron Emir', centralised power, crushed revolts and annexed the Hazarajat, while Britain controlled foreign policy.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a></li></ul></section>
